course: dictionary
lesson: 11296
title: Yak: animal and chatter
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Yak» is a noun for a large animal, and it is also an informal verb meaning to chatter. The words around it are the clues that tell you which one is meant.
explain: «Yaks live on the Tibetan plateau, often above 4000 metres.» Mountains, high and cold, mean the animal: a large, long-haired ox.
explain: «Stop yakking» means stop chattering. Here yakking is a verb, for someone talking on and on about things that do not matter.
fact: A yak is a large, long-haired wild or farm ox.
fact: Yaks live in high, cold mountains, such as Tibet and the Himalayas.
fact: Yaks have long thick hair, a woolly undercoat, and big lungs and hearts that help them in thin air.
fact: To yak is an informal verb that means to chat or talk on and on about unimportant things.
fact: People in the Himalayas use yaks to carry loads and to get milk, butter, meat, wool and dung for fuel.
exercise: «Yaks live on the Tibetan plateau, often above 4000 metres.» Where do yaks live? | Dark forests in cities | Hot deserts by the sea | Deep under the ocean | *High, cold mountains
exercise: «Stop yakking, we need to leave now!» What does yakking mean here? | Eating grass quietly | *Chattering on about unimportant things | Shouting for help | Carrying heavy loads up a hill
exercise: People in the Himalayas get milk, butter and wool from which animal? | Horses | Camels | Reindeer | *Yaks
test: What does a yak's long thick hair help it do? | Walk faster up steep paths | *Keep warm in the cold, thin air of the mountains | Hide from the sun | Swim across rivers
test: Which sentence uses yak as a verb? | «The yak walked slowly up the hill.» | *«Ben yakked on the phone for an hour about nothing.» | «Yaks have big lungs.» | «The yak carried heavy bags.»
exam: Tom says, «Yaks are useful in the mountains,» and then, «Stop yakking!» What is the difference between the two uses of yak? | *The first is a large animal, the second means chattering | Both mean exactly the same thing | The first means chatter, the second is an animal | Neither is a real English word
